  <div class="blue-debrief-label">Debrief — Active Threat Containment</div>
  <div class="blue-debrief-card">
    <h3>Containment Summary</h3>
    <div id="containment-summary"></div>
  </div>
  <div class="blue-debrief-card">
    <h3>Attack Chain Analysis</h3>
    <p>The malware followed a classic lateral movement pattern:</p>
    <p><strong>Stage 1 — Initial Compromise (WS-MKT-042):</strong> Patient zero. The marketing workstation was compromised via a phishing email. The malware spawned a PowerShell process from svchost.exe to establish persistence and begin credential harvesting.</p>
    <p><strong>Stage 2 — Lateral Movement (WS-FIN-017):</strong> Using harvested credentials, the malware connected to the finance workstation via SMB (port 445). It deployed a copy of itself and began enumerating file shares.</p>
    <p><strong>Stage 3 — Staging (SRV-FILE-01):</strong> The file server was the staging ground. The malware copied ransomware payloads to admin shares and began encrypting test files. The next target was the domain controller.</p>
    <p><strong>Stage 4 — Intended Target (SRV-DC-01):</strong> Had the malware reached the domain controller, it would have deployed ransomware to all domain-joined machines via Group Policy. You stopped it.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="blue-debrief-card">
    <h3>Lateral Movement Detection</h3>
    <p><strong>Indicators you should have spotted:</strong></p>
    <p><strong>Process anomalies:</strong> svchost.exe should never spawn powershell.exe. This is a common LOLBIN (living-off-the-land binary) technique attackers use to blend in with legitimate system processes.</p>
    <p><strong>Unusual SMB connections:</strong> Workstation-to-workstation SMB traffic is almost always suspicious. Workstations should connect to file servers, not to each other. Lateral movement tools like PsExec and WMI use SMB.</p>
    <p><strong>Large file staging:</strong> Bulk file copies to admin shares (C$, ADMIN$) during off-hours is a strong indicator of ransomware staging. Legitimate admins use deployment tools, not raw share copies.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="blue-debrief-card">
    <h3>Post-Containment Forensics</h3>
    <p>After isolating infected hosts, the next steps are:</p>
    <p><strong>1. Preserve evidence:</strong> Take memory dumps and disk images of all isolated hosts before remediation. These are critical for root cause analysis and potential legal proceedings.</p>
    <p><strong>2. Credential reset:</strong> All credentials used on infected machines must be considered compromised. Reset passwords for all users who logged into WS-MKT-042, WS-FIN-017, and SRV-FILE-01.</p>
    <p><strong>3. IOC extraction:</strong> Extract malware hashes, C2 domains, and persistence mechanisms from the infected hosts. Use these to scan the rest of the network for additional compromises.</p>
    <p><strong>4. Root cause analysis:</strong> Trace the initial compromise vector (likely phishing email to WS-MKT-042) and implement controls to prevent recurrence.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="blue-debrief-card">
    <h3>Prevention — Stopping Lateral Movement</h3>
    <p><strong>Network Segmentation:</strong> Workstations should not be able to reach each other on port 445 (SMB). Implement micro-segmentation so workstations can only reach servers they need.</p>
    <p><strong>EDR Deployment:</strong> Endpoint Detection and Response agents on all hosts would have caught svchost.exe spawning powershell.exe in real time and auto-isolated the host.</p>
    <p><strong>Least Privilege:</strong> No user account should have admin access to multiple machines. If the compromised credentials only worked on one host, lateral movement would have failed.</p>
    <p><strong>SMB Signing:</strong> Enforcing SMB signing prevents relay attacks and makes lateral movement via SMB significantly harder.</p>
    <p><strong>LAPS (Local Administrator Password Solution):</strong> Randomize local admin passwords so compromising one host doesn't give you access to others.</p>
  </div>
</div>

